26|0@gaming^1438902851#ctu6iv6:Dafoshizinator$:Its actually a promotion for the video game. If you look closely you can see those clear plastic balls. Those are going to be filled with a voucher that wins a copy of the game. The catch is that they are absurdly difficult to pick up so 99% of time when you descend imperfectly the claw slide off and you end up with doritos or nothing. The 1% of the time you hit it dead on it triggers a sensor to clamp the claw but it only has barely enough force to grip the sphere so once again its a game of impossible luck. Now I don't know the actual odds of this machine of course but in the early 2000s arcade games made a flip to offering higher end prizes but therefore increasing the odds of failure by a lot. I remember a sudden switch during my childhood that went from seeing almost entirely pokemon stuffed animals, trading cards, and laser pointers in the seaside heights boardwark arcade machines to seeing impossible to grab vouchers for xboxes and gamecubes (the most insulting ones were the knock of ipods, cause they look valuable like real ipods to the poor dumb passerbys but then you drop like 100 bucks playing and playing on a machine only to win a knockoff worth no more than 5). 
